# Microsoft Enterprise Code Quality Standards

## Enterprise-Grade Tools

- **ESLint**: 142+ enterprise rules ([.eslintrc.json](mdc:.eslintrc.json))
- **Prettier**: Enterprise formatting standards ([.prettierrc.json](mdc:.prettierrc.json))
- **TypeScript**: Strict enterprise mode ([tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json))
- **Husky**: Enterprise git hooks ([.husky/](mdc:.husky))
- **SonarQube**: Enterprise code analysis and security scanning
- **Microsoft Security Code Analysis**: Enterprise security standards

## Scripts

```bash
npm run lint          # Check issues
npm run lint:fix      # Auto-fix
npm run format        # Format code
npm run type-check    # TypeScript check
npm run validate      # All checks
```

## Git Hooks

- **Pre-commit**: lint:fix + format + type-check
- **Pre-push**: lint + type-check + build
- **Commit-msg**: Conventional commits

## Conventions

- **No `any` types** - Use proper TypeScript types
- **Explicit return types** - On all functions
- **Import ordering** - External, internal, utils
- **120 char line width** - For readability
- **Max complexity: 10** - Keep functions simple
- **Max file size: 500 lines** - Split large files

## Naming

- Files: camelCase
- Classes: PascalCase
- Functions: camelCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Interfaces: IPascalCase (with I prefix)

## Commit Format

```
type(scope): subject

Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore
```

## Rules

- ✅ Run `npm run validate` before committing
- ✅ Fix all ESLint errors
- ✅ Follow naming conventions
- ✅ Keep functions under 100 lines
- ✅ Keep files under 500 lines
- ❌ Don't bypass git hooks
- ❌ Don't commit with lint errors
